      "id": "54d19544-7156-58e9-a090-12f8e9492226",
      "title": "Testing Integrating Factors for ODEs in Differential Form"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "40f589ce-b5b7-5ad8-8cde-42bca8504c07",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following inexact differential equation, on a region where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.\n\n$$\n-y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + x\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo test this candidate, multiply both coefficients and check whether the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-2}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A candidate is an integrating factor if multiplying the equation by it gives an exact equation. First multiply both coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x^{3}}\\left(-y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)&=0 \\\\ -\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{y}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the transformed equation,\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe exactness test compares the partial derivatives as functions on the region:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}}\\right)=-\\frac{2 y}{x^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation is exact. Therefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the initial equation.",
