      "title": "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on Y Satisfied"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a895dca0-a91a-53b0-a436-674abf60f70a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find an integrating factor to convert the following differential equation into an **exact equation**. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$$\n\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0,\\qquad y>0\n$$\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nHere $\\exp(y)$ denotes $e^y$.Denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3 y^{2},\\qquad Q(x,y)=- 3 x y\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nFor this equation,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nThis depends on $y$ only, so\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nMultiplying gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\n$$\n\n",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For an equation in differential form, the condition is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=h(y)\n$$\n\nwith $h(y)$ independent of $x$. Then\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)\n$$\n\nSet\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3 y^{2},\\qquad Q(x,y)=- 3 x y\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)=6 y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 3 x y\\right)=- 3 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{3 y^{2}}\\left[\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{3}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratio depends on $y$ only. On the stated domain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(\\frac{3}{y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{y^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $y>0$ permits the logarithm and exponential conversion. Multiplying the original equation by this factor gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{1}{y^{3}}\\right)\\left[\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right]=0\n$$\n\n",
